<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Kullu</h1><p class="st-meta">700 CE – 1846 CE (approx.) · capital Sultanpur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An ancient kingdom of the upper Beas valley, whose 17th-century Raja Jagat Singh founded the Dussehra festival that still draws gods from across the valley to Kullu.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kullu">Kullu</a> appears in early Indian texts as Kuluta, and local tradition traces its Pal dynasty to a founder called Behangamani Pal. The early capital was at Jagatsukh and later Naggar. Firm history begins only in the 16th and 17th centuries, when the Rajas took the name Singh and expanded across the surrounding valleys.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagat-singh-of-kullu">Jagat Singh</a> (r. 1637–1672) moved the capital to Sultanpur, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kullu">Kullu</a> town, and installed the image of Raghunath, making him the kingdom's divine sovereign. The annual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kullu-dussehra">Dussehra</a>, at which village deities are carried to Raghunath's presence, dates from his reign. Kullu controlled trade routes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahaul">Lahaul</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spiti">Spiti</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ladakh">Ladakh</a>, and under Man Singh it reached its greatest extent.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Weakened by succession quarrels, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kullu">Kullu</a> fell under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>, which took direct control in 1840. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-sikh-war">First Anglo-Sikh War</a> it passed to the British in 1846 and became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kangra-state">Kangra</a> district; the royal family kept only the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagir</a> of Rupi. Kullu is now a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh">Himachal Pradesh</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="princely state, 700 CE – 1846 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">princely state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Indian princely states</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:73.63%;width:22.81%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 CE – 1846 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sultanpur</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jagat Singh, Man Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kullui</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kulu, Kullu State</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1273&amp;focus=kingdom-of-kullu" aria-label="Kingdom of Kullu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6847 1213 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Kullu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7167 1469L6918 1285M7167 1469L7364 1601"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6918 1285h0M7364 1601h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7167 1469h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7167 1469h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sultanpur. <a href="/atlas?year=1273&amp;focus=kingdom-of-kullu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

